# RFC-0001: Architecture

Status: Active

## Abstract

Raytha is one ASP.NET Core host over four projects. Dependencies point inward:
`Raytha.Web` → `Raytha.Infrastructure` → `Raytha.Application` → `Raytha.Domain`.
Postgres is the only supported database.

## 1. Layers

| Project | Owns | MUST NOT reference |
|---|---|---|
| `src/Raytha.Domain` | Entities, value objects, domain events, domain exceptions | Any other layer; EF Core, Npgsql, ASP.NET Core |
| `src/Raytha.Application` | Commands, queries, handlers, validators, DTOs, interfaces, pipeline behaviors | Infrastructure, Web, Npgsql, Dapper, ASP.NET Core |
| `src/Raytha.Infrastructure` | EF Core `RaythaDbContext`, migrations, file storage, background tasks, feature-flag store, health checks, Raytha Functions runtime | Web |
| `src/Raytha.Web` | Public controllers and views, admin API, admin SPA hosting, REST API v1, middleware, observability | — |

- `Raytha.Application` MAY reference EF Core abstractions, because
  `IRaythaDbContext` exposes `DbSet<T>`. The Npgsql provider and Dapper are
  infrastructure-only.
- Serilog, OpenTelemetry, and Sentry MUST stay in `Raytha.Web`.
- `src/admin` is the React admin workspace (RFC-0006). It is not a .NET layer.

`tests/Raytha.Architecture.Tests/LayerDependencyTests.cs` enforces every row
above. A new package reference that breaks a boundary fails the build, not
review.

## 2. Composition

- Each layer exposes one `ConfigureServices.cs` extension
  (`AddApplicationServices`, `AddInfrastructureServices`, `AddWebUIServices`).
  Registration for a layer's types MUST live there, not in `Startup`.
- `Startup.Configure` owns middleware order. The order that matters:
  exception handler → status-code pages → HTTPS/HSTS (non-Development) → admin
  SPA dev proxy → static files → security headers → admin API JSON guard →
  routing → rate limiter → authentication → authorization → endpoints.
- Cross-layer contracts live in `Raytha.Application/Common/Interfaces`. The
  Application layer defines the interface; Infrastructure implements it.

## 3. Postgres Only

- Postgres 17 is the only supported database. SQL Server support was removed in
  2.0. New code MUST NOT reintroduce a provider abstraction, conditional
  provider branches, or a second set of migrations.
- Provider-specific SQL (`jsonb`, `ILIKE`, `citext`, the JSON query engine)
  belongs in `Raytha.Infrastructure` only.
- Reference SQL for manual installs and upgrades lives in `db/Postgres/`
  (RFC-0012).

## 4. Migrations

- EF migrations MUST live in `src/Raytha.Infrastructure/Persistence/Migrations`.

```bash
dotnet ef migrations add v2_1_0 \
  --project src/Raytha.Infrastructure \
  --startup-project src/Raytha.Web
```

- A migration is named after the release that carries it (`v1_5_0`, `v2_0_0`),
  not after what it changes.
- `APPLY_PENDING_MIGRATIONS=true` applies pending migrations at startup. It is
  the default for Docker Compose and `./tools/dev.sh`; production deployments
  MAY instead run the generated SQL script.
- Adding a migration bumps MINOR. See RFC-0012.

## 5. Health and Configuration

- `/healthz` is liveness and runs no checks. `/healthz/ready` runs checks
  tagged `HealthCheckTags.Ready` (Postgres, file storage). New dependencies
  that would block serving traffic MUST add a readiness check with that tag.
- Configuration keys are read through `IConfiguration` in `Startup` or a typed
  options class; secrets MUST NOT be committed. `.env.example` documents every
  supported environment variable.
